KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp23
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ if consteval: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
g++ and clang++ (GCC 16 for C++23 and C++26) accept this program, and it fails
only its WRONG assertion.  [stmt.if]/4: the first substatement of `if
consteval' is executed in an immediate function context or during manifestly
constant evaluation.  CBMC takes the else branch in the constant evaluation of
`constexpr int c = f()' (c is 2); at run time it takes it correctly.
